  ],
  "text": "Modern concrete lasts about a century. Roman harbour concrete has stood two thousand years underwater. Seawater is what destroys concrete. It is the worst place you could possibly put it. The Romans put volcanic ash into the mix. When seawater gets in, it reacts with that ash and grows new crystals inside the material. So the sea does not wear it away. The sea builds it. There is more. The mix carries lumps of quicklime that were never properly blended in. A crack lets water reach a lump, it reacts, and the crack fills itself back in. We did not lose this recipe to some mystery. We chose differently. Modern concrete sets in hours, costs less, and takes steel. Roman concrete does none of that. We traded two thousand years of life for being able to build it by Friday. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
